# The alpha deployment's environment. Copy to `infra/deploy/env/alpha.env`, which is
# git-ignored, and pass it with `--env-file`:
#
#   cp infra/deploy/env/alpha.env.example infra/deploy/env/alpha.env
#   chmod 600 infra/deploy/env/alpha.env
#   $EDITOR infra/deploy/env/alpha.env
#   infra/deploy/deploy.sh --env-file infra/deploy/env/alpha.env
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS IS NOT `.env`, AND THAT IS THE POINT.
#
# The repository's `.env` is parsed by the Makefile against a strict ALLOWLIST of exactly
# the fifteen names FF-01 section 3 freezes (Makefile, `FROZEN_ENV_NAMES`). Any other name
# is refused by `make` with an explicit error -- the comment there states the intent:
# `.env` "does not carry tool options, credentials for other systems, or anything that
# selects what code runs". `src/auditmanager/bootstrap/settings.py` records the same rule
# for the provider names.
#
# So `AUDITMANAGER_API_TOKEN`, `AUDITMANAGER_PROVIDER_MODE` and the rest of the
# application-layer names cannot live in `.env`, and putting them there would break
# `make gate` rather than configure anything. This file is their channel. It is read by
# `docker compose`, never by `make`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THIS FILE IS ALSO DATA, NOT CODE, and it is worth saying here rather than only in the
# root `.env.example` -- `W45-JUDGE-X` found the warning lived there and not here, which
# means the file an operator actually copies for a deployment never carried it.
# `infra/deploy/deploy.sh`, `reset.sh` and `verify-deployed.sh` all read `alpha.env` the
# same way: `sed` extracts `NAME=VALUE` lines and the file is never sourced, so nothing in
# it can execute. A value with a shell metacharacter is inert text, not a command; a `$(...)`
# in a password stays four literal characters. Quoting follows the same rule this
# repository's other environment files use -- a value either holds no quote character at
# all, or is wrapped in exactly one matching pair of single or double quotes, and there is
# no escaping.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# VALUES BELOW ARE EXAMPLES AND EVERY SECRET IN THEM IS DISPOSABLE. They are the values
# `W14-PKG` brought the stack up with on the development host. Change every one before any
# deployment reachable by anything, and do not reuse them anywhere real.
#
# CHANGING A SECRET MEANS CHANGING IT EVERYWHERE IT IS WRITTEN. `POSTGRES_PASSWORD`,
# `MINIO_ROOT_USER` and `MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD` are each embedded a SECOND time below, inside
# `MIGRATOR_DATABASE_URL`, `S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID` and `S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` -- `deploy.sh`'s
# `derived-secrets-coherent` guard refuses, before any build, if the two sides disagree
# (`D-103`). Rotating only the four names its `placeholder-secrets` guard checks is not
# enough. The application role's password, `ALPHA_APP_DB_PASSWORD`, is embedded a second time inside
# `DATABASE_URL` and is checked the same way by `apply-grants.sh --config-only`, which `deploy.sh` asks.

# --- what this normative-corpus release does and does not configure ----------------
# `deploy.sh` builds the repository-owned PostgreSQL derivative, then runs the one-shot
# `migrate` service before the API. This release creates the corpus/chunk tables plus the
# durable pgvector projection and advances the database to `0013_norm_embeddings`
# automatically. There is no migration command to add here.
#
# There is deliberately NO CORPUS PATH, SNAPSHOT KEY, PGVECTOR OR EMBEDDING VARIABLE in this
# file. The current corpus loader is a DB-only operator command whose filesystem inputs are
# explicit CLI arguments; deploy does not import local corpus bytes, and the API/UI do not query
# the new projection yet. pgvector 0.8.6 and the selected BGE-M3 profile are immutable image/DB
# constants, while model inference is not a runtime dependency. Adding plausible-looking
# variables here would silently configure nothing.
#
# MinIO below remains the alpha application's existing blob store. Immutable custody of each
# normative source PDF and crop is designed but not wired in this release; do not upload them by
# inventing object keys or treating paths/checksums as identity.

# --- instance identity ---------------------------------------------------------
# Compose project, network and ALL THREE named volumes derive from this, so two stacks on
# one host never share live state. Same rule as FF-01 section 5, one layer up.
#
# Three since `R-51` added `<instance>-web-sessions` beside `-postgres-data` and `-s3-data`,
# and the third is the one holding reviewer credentials. This line said "BOTH" until
# 2026-09-29 (`Y9`). Ask the compose file rather than this comment:
#
#   docker compose --env-file infra/deploy/env/alpha.env \
#     -f infra/deploy/compose.server.yml config --volumes
ALPHA_INSTANCE=auditmanager-alpha

# --- running it twice ----------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL, AND `yes` WHEN IT IS ABSENT. `deploy.sh` compares what a build produced with
# the image the name pointed at before it -- by the layer digests and the runtime
# configuration, never by the id, which BuildKit moves on every build -- and when they are
# the same it points the name back at the image that was already there. A second run
# against an unchanged tree then replaces no container at all (`D-36`).
#
# `no` is the behaviour before `W24-IDEM`: every build's image is new, and `api`, `web`
# and `migrate` are recreated on every run. Nothing else changes, and an operator loses
# nothing but that. Any OTHER value is refused before anything is built rather than read
# as the default.
#ALPHA_PRESERVE_IMAGE_IDENTITY=yes

# --- the one published port, and the interface it is published ON ---------------
# `T-2`: the reverse proxy is the only thing that leaves this host. The API, the web app,
# PostgreSQL and the object store are reachable only on the compose network.
ALPHA_HTTP_PORT=8080

# OPTIONAL. `127.0.0.1` when absent, which is the safe value, and that default is the
# whole point of the variable existing. Ruled by the owner 2026-09-22, closing `D-49`.
#
# The compose file used to publish this port with no interface at all, which binds
# `0.0.0.0`. That was measured rather than supposed: on 2026-09-22 the stand answered `200` on
# the host's PUBLIC address, on `/bff/v1`, which then served EVERY operation of this surface
# INCLUDING WRITES to anyone, because the BFF added the credential server-side. THAT IS
# HISTORY: since wave 34 the BFF forwards a credential only for a live session cookie and
# answers `401` to every other caller, and `/api/v1/openapi.json` answers `401` since `R-31`
# (`docs/program/EXPOSURE-REVIEW-01.md` `E-8`). The default below stays as defence in depth.
# `ufw` did not stop it and structurally cannot: a Docker-published port never reaches
# ufw's `INPUT` chain, because Docker writes its own `nat/DOCKER` and `filter/DOCKER` rules
# and those are traversed first. `ufw status` reported one open port while this one answered
# the world.
#
# Reach a loopback-bound stand with a tunnel:
#
#   ssh -L 31500:127.0.0.1:31500 <host>        then open http://127.0.0.1:31500
#
# SET THIS ONLY ON A HOST THAT IS GENUINELY MEANT TO PUBLISH THE PLAIN APPLICATION PORT — one
# behind a firewall you have verified reaches Docker's chains. It governs ONLY that plain
# listener. Since `PROD-01-TLS-ACME-01` the public ports of a stack that terminates TLS itself
# (`ALPHA_CERT_NAME`, below) are 80 and 443, governed by `ALPHA_PUBLIC_ADDRESS`, and with a name
# set `deploy.sh` REFUSES a non-loopback value here: the application is public only over TLS.
# `R-4` puts real client documents on the pilot server; this is the line that decides who can
# reach them.
#ALPHA_BIND_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0

# --- who may tell the proxy the client's address -----------------------------------
# OPTIONAL, AND EMPTY WHEN ABSENT, which trusts nobody. `REAL-IP-EDGE-01`, debts `D-144` and
# `D-191` `E-3`.
#
# Every per-address throttle (sign-up, sign-in token exchange, the web tier's guest bucket)
# counts the address of the peer that connected to the proxy container. When the TLS terminator
# is ANOTHER nginx on the host, that peer is always the same one -- the compose network's
# gateway -- so the throttles are one shared bucket for every visitor. Naming the terminator
# here makes the proxy read the visitor's address from the `X-Forwarded-For` the terminator
# writes, and from nobody else's.
#
# A comma- or space-separated list of IPv4/IPv6 addresses or CIDRs, as narrow as you can make
# them (the single gateway address, `/32`). `0.0.0.0/0` and host names are refused. For a host
# nginx in front of a loopback-published port the value is the gateway of THIS instance's
# network, which is not the same on every host:
#
#   docker network inspect <ALPHA_INSTANCE>-net \
#     --format '{{(index .IPAM.Config 0).Gateway}}'        # e.g. 172.29.0.1  ->  172.29.0.1/32
#
# Set it ONLY together with the matching terminator line (`proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For
# $remote_addr;`), and read `infra/deploy/README.md`, "The client address", first: a trusted
# peer that forwards a client-written header unchanged lets that client choose its bucket.
# Leave it empty when no other proxy sits in front of this one. With `ALPHA_CERT_NAME` set below there is
# no other proxy, and `deploy.sh` REFUSES a non-empty value (guard `trusted-proxies-empty-with-name`).
#ALPHA_TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS=

# --- the TLS terminator: a certificate name, and everything that follows from it -
# `PROD-01-TLS-ACME-01`, `ADR-0021` section 2, `R-146a`, `R-146aa`, `R-146ac`, `R-146s`.
#
# EMPTY OR ABSENT IS THE DEFAULT AND CHANGES NOTHING: no ACME client, no public port, the stack
# exactly as it was before these lines existed. That is also the rollback -- empty the name and
# deploy. SET, the stack gets its own TLS terminator: an ACME client as a service of this compose
# project, public port 80 for the challenge and a redirect (no application route behind it), and
# public port 443 for the application over TLS, with HSTS and a `Secure` session cookie.
# `docs/program/DEPLOYMENT_RUNBOOK.md` section 6 is the numbered procedure, including the staging
# rehearsal that comes before the first real issuance and the cutover from the host's nginx.
#
# THE NAME IS THE PUBLIC DNS NAME OF THIS HOST, which a certificate authority must be able to
# resolve to it. The value below is a placeholder that `deploy.sh` refuses by shape (a reserved
# `.invalid` name); the live name belongs in the untracked `alpha.env` on the host and in no
# default anywhere in the repository. Lower case, at least two labels, no wildcard, no port.
#ALPHA_CERT_NAME=host.example.invalid

# A contact address the authority may write to about an expiring certificate. OPTIONAL: empty
# registers without one. Printed by `docker compose config`; it is not a secret.
#ALPHA_ACME_EMAIL=

# `yes` (the default, and what an absent line means) asks the authority's TEST endpoint, whose
# certificates no browser trusts and whose rate limits are loose: the rehearsal. `no` is the
# real issuance and counts against the authority's limits -- shared with every other user of the
# name's zone if the name is under `sslip.io` (`R-146ac`), so rehearse first. `deploy.sh` prints
# which one it is on every run, `verify-deployed.sh` FAILS on a staging certificate unless it is
# given `--acme-rehearsal`, and `readiness.sh` reports it.
#ALPHA_ACME_STAGING=yes

# `yes` asks for public port 443 -- for a deploy whose certificate already exists. `no` (the
# default) keeps it closed. THIS IS THE OPERATOR'S HALF OF THE DECISION, and the credential gate
# of `R-146s` is the other half (`PROD-02`): a deploy publishes 443 only when this says `yes`, a
# pair exists AND `python -m auditmanager.access.check --gate` finds the published `admin` /
# `password` pair NOT valid. While it is valid the deploy ends non-zero naming the login and the
# stack stays on loopback (sign in through an SSH tunnel -- `ssh -L 31500:127.0.0.1:31500 <host>`
# -- change the password, deploy again), and a restore that brings the pair back (`reset.sh
# --restore`, `copy-serve.sh restore-db`) closes 443 again. A temporary password an administrator
# issued is a warning line and never closes anything (`R-146z`).
#ALPHA_PUBLISH_HTTPS=no

# The interface the two PUBLIC ports bind: every interface unless the host has several addresses
# and one is meant. Not `ALPHA_BIND_ADDRESS`, which stays the plain application listener's.
#ALPHA_PUBLIC_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0

# The challenge port, 80 unless this host's port 80 is not free to this stack -- a rehearsal on a
# development machine. The authority always asks port 80 of the public name.
#ALPHA_ACME_HTTP_PORT=80

# The published HTTPS port, 443 unless this host says otherwise. It is read only for a deploy that
# publishes it (`ALPHA_PUBLISH_HTTPS=yes`, a certificate that exists and a clean credential gate).
#ALPHA_HTTPS_PORT=443

# THE CERTIFICATE AND ITS KEY ARE NOT NAMED HERE, and that is deliberate: they live in a named
# volume the ACME client writes and the proxy mounts read-only, and no variable carries them, so
# `docker compose config` cannot print one. When the host's nginx stops being the terminator, empty
# `ALPHA_TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS` above as well (the real client address then arrives on the connection);
# `deploy.sh` refuses a name together with a non-empty list.

# --- PostgreSQL, inside the stack: TWO ROLES -----------------------------------
# `PROD-04-DB-ROLES-01`, `R-146n` item 1, `AD-02`, `ADR-0021` section 3.
#
# The MIGRATOR is `POSTGRES_USER`, the role the postgres image creates at first start. It owns every object, runs
# `migrate` and `release-notes`, runs the operator tools that need write-everything (the product purge, the project
# erase), and is a SUPERUSER because the image's bootstrap role is one and migration `0013` needs one (`CREATE
# EXTENSION vector`). Its url is `MIGRATOR_DATABASE_URL`, below, and it reaches only the one-shot services and the
# operator wrappers -- never `api` or `worker`.
POSTGRES_DB=auditmanager_alpha
POSTGRES_USER=auditmanager_alpha
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me-disposable-postgres-password

# The APPLICATION role is the one `api` and `worker` connect as (`DATABASE_URL`, below). It can log in and it can
# read and write the tables the contexts use; it cannot change the schema, cannot truncate, owns nothing, and cannot
# update or delete a row of an append-only table. `infra/deploy/apply-grants.sh` creates it and applies its rights
# after every `migrate` and every restore -- `deploy.sh`, `reset.sh` and `copy-serve.sh restore-db` run it.
#
# THE SECOND PASSWORD IS YOURS TO CHOOSE ON THE HOST. This file ships no value for it and `deploy.sh` REFUSES an
# empty one (`[app_db_password_unset]`); there is no fall-back to the owner role. 20 to 128 characters from
# A-Z a-z 0-9 . _ ~ - (it is written into a URL and a command without quoting), and not the owner's password:
#
#   head -c 24 /dev/urandom | od -An -tx1 | tr -d ' \n'; echo
#
# Put the same value in `ALPHA_APP_DB_PASSWORD` and inside `DATABASE_URL` below. Rotating it is: change both, deploy
# (the step sets the new password; the old one stops working).
ALPHA_APP_DB_USER=auditmanager_alpha_app
ALPHA_APP_DB_PASSWORD=

# --- object storage, inside the stack ------------------------------------------
# MinIO refuses a root password shorter than 8 characters.
MINIO_ROOT_USER=change-me-disposable-minio-root
MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD=change-me-disposable-minio-password
S3_BUCKET=auditmanager-alpha

# --- what the application reads ------------------------------------------------
# Host names are compose service names, not 127.0.0.1: the application runs in a container
# beside them. `settings.load` refuses to start if any of these is missing.
# The APPLICATION role's url: ALPHA_APP_DB_USER and ALPHA_APP_DB_PASSWORD, above. The password below is a
# placeholder that `apply-grants.sh --config-only` refuses until you replace it with ALPHA_APP_DB_PASSWORD.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://auditmanager_alpha_app:replace-with-ALPHA_APP_DB_PASSWORD@postgres:5432/auditmanager_alpha
# The OWNER's url: POSTGRES_USER and POSTGRES_PASSWORD, above. Only `migrate`, `release-notes` and the operator
# tools that need write-everything receive it (`compose.server.yml`, `purge-product-data.sh`).
MIGRATOR_DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://auditmanager_alpha:change-me-disposable-postgres-password@postgres:5432/auditmanager_alpha
S3_ENDPOINT_URL=http://s3:9000
S3_REGION=us-east-1
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=change-me-disposable-minio-root
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=change-me-disposable-minio-password

# --- `T-6`'s authorization credential ------------------------------------------
# THE LOAD-BEARING ONE. `W13_CLOSURE.md` section 7: the authorization seam is FAIL-CLOSED.
# An application with no token configured answers `authentication_required` to every one of
# its operations while `/healthz` and `/readyz` stay green -- from a browser that
# looks like a broken product rather than an unconfigured one.
#
# `Y10`: an operation count stood here and was true when it was written. The W48 prose guard
# now discovers every tracked deployment text file through Git rather than a suffix allow-list,
# so this `.example` is inside its scope. `deploy.sh` also prints the live value on every run,
# from the contract and from the running process:
#
#   grep -n 'frozen ops\|served ops' <your deploy log>      # frozen ops : 20 / served ops : 20
#
# Since `W14-PKG` it is required AT CONSTRUCTION (`bootstrap/settings.py`,
# `API_TOKEN_ENV`), so a container started without it exits non-zero at once instead of
# serving refusals. That is the Gate C contract `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` is already held to.
#
# Generate one per deployment and never reuse this example:
#
#   python3 -c 'import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))'
#
# THE BROWSER PRESENTS NOTHING, AND THIS IS NOT A BEARER TOKEN. `Y6`: until 2026-09-29
# this line read "The browser client presents it as `Authorization: Bearer <token>`", and
# it was false twice over. The web tier forwards server-side and the value never reaches a
# browser (`W15-AUTH`); and since wave 34 this is the key the API *signs* reviewer
# credentials with (`src/auditmanager/api/security.py`, `_SIGNING_CONTEXT`), not a token
# anyone presents -- presented as a bearer it answers 401, driven by `W47-JUDGE-X` §1.4.
# `infra/deploy/README.md` was corrected when the code changed and this file, the one a
# human copies and edits, was not.
AUDITMANAGER_API_TOKEN=change-me-this-example-token-authorizes-nothing

# --- provider selection --------------------------------------------------------
# `recorded` replays fixtures and cannot spend money. `live` and `proxy` both need a
# credential, and the credential does NOT go in this file -- see provider.env.example.
AUDITMANAGER_PROVIDER_MODE=recorded

# --- executor connections (`EXEC-HTTP-01`, `R-143`) ---------------------------------
# Where a run's model adapter comes from. `environment` (the default) is the path every stand has
# run until now: `PROXY_LLM_*` or `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` from provider.env. `database` uses the
# connections an administrator manages under «Исполнители»: a run freezes the connection at start.
# `database` requires AUDITMANAGER_PROVIDER_MODE=live. A complete PROXY_LLM_* triplet may stay
# in the worker-only provider.env: the worker creates one baseline OpenRouter-compatible
# connection and queues its probe on first start. It never changes an existing assignment.
# The API does not load provider.env. See the runbook section «Подключения».
AUDITMANAGER_PROVIDER_SOURCE=environment
# The PUBLIC half of the key ring that seals a connection's key (`kid:base64`, comma-separated), and
# the key id new keys are sealed to. Public, so they belong here and reach both the `api` and the
# `worker`; the api seals to them and can never open a key. The PRIVATE ring is not here: it is
# AUDITMANAGER_SECRETS_KEYS in provider.env, read by the worker alone. Without a public key
# `setExecutorSecret` answers `dependency_unavailable` and nothing is stored in the clear.
AUDITMANAGER_SECRETS_PUBLIC_KEYS=
AUDITMANAGER_SECRETS_ACTIVE_KID=
# The sealed reviewed AR v3 beta pack (`AR-V3-BETA-PACK-INSTALL-01`). Blank installs no pack, and
# starting the six-scope group then refuses with `dependency_unavailable`. To install: seal the
# manifest with `python -m auditmanager.runs.v3_pack_cli seal`, copy it to
# `infra/deploy/ar-v3-pack/` (mounted read-only at `/run/ar-v3`), set this to
# `/run/ar-v3/<file>` and restart `api` and `worker`. A value that names a missing or invalid
# file stops both services at start; it never falls back to "no pack". Runbook:
# `docs/program/AR-V3-BETA-PACK-INSTALL-01.md`. The file holds no secret.
AR_V3_BETA_PACK_MANIFEST=

# --- how many runs the worker executes at once (`EXEC-PAR-QUEUE-01`) ----------------
# A whole number from 1 to 16; read by the `worker` service only. `1` is one run at a time, the
# behaviour before this setting existed, and setting it back to `1` (or removing the line) is the
# rollback. A value that is not a plain whole number in range stops the worker at start; it is
# never clamped or replaced by `1`. Whatever it is, one executor connection has at most ONE active
# run: a second run for a busy connection waits in the queue and holds no slot, so the next queued
# run for a free connection runs instead. A run without a connection is not limited. More slots
# mean more database connections and more simultaneous model calls on one host: raise it
# deliberately, and restart `worker` for it to take effect (it drains first).
AUDITMANAGER_WORKER_SLOTS=1

# --- memory limits (`ERASE-WRAP-01` for `PROD-05b`; `ADR-0021` section 4, `D-166`) ------------------
# One variable per long-running service (`compose.server.yml`: `mem_limit: ${ALPHA_MEM_LIMIT_<SERVICE>:-0}`). ALL SIX ARE
# EMPTY HERE, and empty (or absent) means NO LIMIT -- Docker reads `0` as unlimited -- which is how the stack ran before
# these lines existed. No number is shipped because the measurement they need has not been taken: `PROD-05a` is
# `docker stats --no-stream` on the real host during a 500-page analysis (`R-146ad`: the host has 4 GB), and a limit set
# below the worker's footprint kills it in the middle of a run. Measure first, then set each value above the measured peak
# by a margin you write down, and keep their sum below the host's RAM minus what runs outside the containers. The
# runbook section 19 has the procedure and how to read an out-of-memory kill. A value is what `docker run --memory`
# takes: a whole number of bytes, or a whole number with a unit `b`, `k`, `m` or `g` (`1536m`, `2g`). Check a new value
# with `docker compose --env-file ... --file ... config` before deploying. A change takes effect when `deploy.sh` (or
# `docker compose up -d`) recreates the service.
ALPHA_MEM_LIMIT_POSTGRES=
ALPHA_MEM_LIMIT_S3=
ALPHA_MEM_LIMIT_API=
ALPHA_MEM_LIMIT_WORKER=
ALPHA_MEM_LIMIT_WEB=
ALPHA_MEM_LIMIT_PROXY=

# --- the web build ---------------------------------------------------------------
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` values are compiled into the browser bundle, so nothing secret may be
# named here. `/api/v1` is RELATIVE by design (`T-2`): the image names no origin and works
# behind whatever host the proxy answers on.
# The browser talks to the Next server, which holds the credential and forwards to the API.
# Still relative, so the image names no origin. `/api/v1` is what the *server* calls, not
# what the browser does -- see `W15-AUTH`'s review for why the token cannot go the other way.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=/bff/v1
NEXT_PUBLIC_INSTANCE_LABEL=alpha

# Enable version/stage comparison explicitly for a development presentation.
# Empty preserves the default exposure policy. Applied to API and worker.
AUDITMANAGER_COMPARISON_MODULES=
# Fresh estimates include bounded block/image review; 0 disables fresh plans only.
AUDITMANAGER_EXTENDED_ANALYSIS=1
